# Annotation volume geometry

## The model
- A location is stored in **mosaic** (section) space: `MosaicShape`, `X`, `Y`, `Radius`, `Width`. Mosaic is the truth. On
  the gRPC client `LocationObj.MosaicShape` and `VolumeShape` are `IShape2D` (a circle is a `Geometry.Circle`).
- `VolumeShape`, `VolumeX`, `VolumeY` (and the volume radius inside a circle's `VolumeShape`) are a **cache**: the mosaic
  geometry mapped through a section-to-volume transform.
- A changed `.stos` must re-render every annotation at once, so nothing that draws may trust the cache.

## The cache basis: the default transform (`VolumeCache`)
- The persisted cache **always** holds the volume's **default** transform, whatever transform is on screen, so server
  queries, OData and export see one space. Its value is `VolumeCache.Shape(location, default)`: the forward owner under
  the default transform, a circle as its exact `Geometry.Circle` (`CacheShape`; the smoothed ring is the inscribed circle
  of its polygon, about 2% smaller), any other type as `Shape`.
- The default transform comes from one helper: `IDefaultVolumeTransformProvider.GetDefaultSectionToVolumeTransform`
  (`VolumeViewModel` in Viking; `VolumeTransformProvider` in Jotunn). `VolumeCache.Basis(location, transform)` resolves it
  for a save; Viking's `AnnotationOverlay` and `AnnotationBootstrap` (Jotunn) both register
  `LocationVolumeGeometryExtensions.DefaultBasisProvider` as `VolumeCache.DefaultTransforms`; without an open volume
  (VikingAU, unit tests) the transform passed is the basis, and the caller must pass the default.
- Jotunn draws annotations in the transform its tiles use: `AnnotationScene.SetVolumeTransform` sets the shared
  `AnnotationBootstrap.Transforms.TransformName` from the section list's active transform and rebuilds the section views.
- Policies, documented on `VolumeCache`: a section the volume does not align (the reference section, a
  `NonStosSectionNumbers` section: no entry in the default) maps by identity, as the viewer and `MappingManager` place it;
  a default transform that did not load at all while others did leaves **no basis** (`GetDefaultSectionToVolumeTransform`
  null): the save is refused with `VolumeCache.NoBasisMessage` and the refresh stops. Neither identity nor the transform on
  screen is ever written instead. (On this client a `.stos` that fails to load fails the volume load, so Legacy's
  per-section `Volume.StosLoadFailed` is not needed.)
  A location the default cannot map exactly gets the owner's failure policy (extrapolated), so the refresh writes it
  rather than leaving a stale cache. The client never sends a null `VolumeShape`: the WCF server would store a circle of
  the mosaic radius at the old `VolumePosition`.
- **After a `.stos` change, refresh the cache** with **Update Volume Positions** (`VolumePositionUpdateJob`, Annotation
  menu, review access; it reads and writes each section through its own gRPC client) or VikingAU. Both write
  `VolumeCache.Shape` under the default transform, exactly what a save writes; the job has no transform picker.

## The forward owner: `LocationVolumeGeometry` (SqlGeometryAnnotationExtensions)
`LocationVolumeGeometry.For(location, transform)` (or `For(location, provider)`) is the only place mosaic geometry is
mapped forward for drawing. It carries:
- `Shape`: `MapShape(type, mosaic, transform)` = `GetSmoothedShape(TryMapShapeSectionToVolume(mosaic))`.
  `CacheShape` is what the stored cache holds: `Shape`, except a circle is its exact `Circle` (the smoothed ring is the
  inscribed circle of its polygon, about 2% smaller).
- `ControlPoints` / `ControlPolygon()`: the mapped, unsmoothed control points (edit handles, polylines, polygons).
- `Circle` (circles, points): centre from `X,Y`, radius by the five-point mean (`circle-mapping-scale.mdc`).
- `Scale`: the local five-point mean scale. `Width` = mosaic width x `Scale`; `Radius` = mosaic radius x `Scale` (the
  circle's own radius for circles). Line and curve widths scale exactly as circle radii do.
- `Center` (labels, markers, link previews), `AdjacentMarker(scalar)` (the circle drawn on an adjacent section: the
  inscribed circle of the mapped shape for polygon-likes), `Outline` (the polygon polygon-likes are hit-tested as and
  links leave), `InterpolationPoints` (what curve views draw with, so drawn == hit-tested).

It is cached per location, keyed by the `MosaicShape` instance (the `IShape2D`, converted to SQL geometry once by
`LocationVolumeGeometry.ToSqlGeometry`, which also takes a point's `Vector2`), width, type and the transform **object** (a
reloaded `.stos` is a new object), so a shape edit or a transform change rebuilds it. Up to three geometries are kept per
location, one per transform object, so a save under the default transform does not evict the one drawn under a
non-default active transform. A transform provider must therefore
hand out the same object for a section until its `.stos` changes (`VolumeViewModel`'s mappings; `VolumeTransformProvider`
keeps one per section). One documented mapping-failure policy lives
on the class (circles extrapolate the centre and keep the mosaic radius; other shapes extrapolate with scale 1;
`CanDrawOnSection` is false for extrapolated polygons and polylines). Do not add a second policy in a view.

## Views
- Views (`View/`, `ViewModel/`) take a `LocationVolumeGeometry`; `AnnotationViewFactory` builds it. Views never receive or
  call a transform: no `SectionToVolume(`, `TrySectionToVolume(`, `TryMapShapeSectionToVolume(`.
- Views never read the stored cache: no `.VolumePosition`, `.VolumeShape`, `VolumeX/VolumeY` for drawing or hit-testing.
  Code with a location but no transform uses `LocationVolumeGeometryExtensions` (`VolumeGeometry()`, `VolumeCenter()`,
  `VolumeRadius()`), which reads the open volume's transforms (Jotunn's `AnnotationBootstrap.Transforms`, else Viking's
  open volume).
- A link, label or marker that belongs to a location takes its geometry from that location's owner, so it meets what is
  drawn.
- `VolumeGeometryGuardTests` enforces this for `View/`, `ViewModel/`, `UI/` (commands, actions, auto-polygonize) and
  `Tools/` (the tool layer Jotunn draws previews and edits through: tools save through the same owners); each
  exception is a read-only entry on its named, commented allow-list. It also flags any `.MosaicShape =` write in
  WebAnnotation, VikingAU and SqlGeometryAnnotationExtensions outside `LocationObjExtensions`. `LocationVolumeGeometryTests` checks every view and
  every factory branch against the owner (property and closed-set tests); add a new view there.

## Commands and edits: the inverse rule (in force)
The user edits in volume space (what is on screen). Every command and save path follows one rule:
1. **Start from the forward owner.** A command edits the geometry `LocationVolumeGeometry.For` gives (`ControlPoints`,
   `ControlPolygon()`, `Circle`, `Width`), never the stored `VolumeShape` and never its own mapping of the mosaic shape.
   Previews of geometry that is not a saved location's (a polygon being moved, a prompt, a cut piece) use the owner's
   static forward helpers (`TryMapPoints`, `TryMapPolygon`, `TryMapCircle`, `TryMapControlShape`, `MapShape`).
2. **Map the edit back with the one inverse owner**, `LocationVolumeGeometry.ToMosaic` (shape, control points, polygon or
   circle, with an optional volume width) and its point-level forms `TryMapPointsToMosaic`, `TryMapPolygonToMosaic`,
   `TryMapPolylineToMosaic`. Points map with the checked inverse, all or none. A circle's mosaic radius is solved against
   the forward five-point mean and a width is the volume width divided by the forward owner's local scale, so
   forward(inverse(v)) == v, also under shear and non-rigid warps.
3. **Save mosaic, derive volume.** `LocationObjExtensions.TryApply` (or `SetShapeFromGeometryInSection` for an edit made in
   mosaic space, such as a polygon move, a hole removal or Simplify Polygon) sets `MosaicShape` (and `Width`) and writes
   `VolumeShape` as `VolumeCache.Shape` under the **default** transform. The edit is checked and mapped back under the
   transform on screen; only the cache uses the default. **No code writes `MosaicShape` directly**, and no code builds a
   location from a volume shape it kept (`new LocationObj(parent, mosaic, volume, …)`): a command never copies a volume
   value into the mosaic columns and never keeps the user's volume shape.
4. **Failure is explicit.** When any point does not map, `MosaicGeometry.Failure` says why; the command refuses, the
   location keeps its shape, and the user is shown the reason (`LocationActions.ShowUnmappedEdit`). An edit attempted on a
   location the owner cannot map exactly (`IsExact` false) starts no command and shows `LocationActions.UneditableMessage`
   (`EditableGeometry(loc, transform, reportTo)`). Nothing partial or extrapolated is saved as mosaic.
   - Exception, by design: a pen choice (cut piece, linked location, contour change) whose points cannot be mapped is not
     offered and is only traced, since the user never picked it.

Mosaic-first edits that keep stored control points (pen splice cuts, region cuts, hole removal, polygon moves by the
mapped drag) stay mosaic-first and map only their new points back.
